\fill (C) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (D) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (E) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[below right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[above left] at (D) {$D$};
\node[above] at (E) {$E$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Explain why Mia's reasoning is not enough, and state whether this circuit is planar.

development.solution_text:
Planarity is a property of the network itself, not of one picture of it, so crossings in a single drawing prove nothing.
A graph is non-planar only when **every** possible drawing has a crossing.
Moving $E$ around to the outside keeps all seven tracks and removes both crossings.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3.0,0);
\coordinate (C) at (3.0,2.4);
\coordinate (D) at (0,2.4);
\coordinate (E) at (4.6,-2.2);
\draw (A)--(B);
\draw (B)--(C);
\draw (C)--(D);
\draw (D)--(A);
\draw (B)--(D);
\draw (E)--(A);
\draw (E)--(C);
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (C) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (D) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (E) circle (2.5pt);
\node[left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[below right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[above right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[above left] at (D) {$D$};
\node[below right] at (E) {$E$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
**Mia has judged the drawing instead of the network; the circuit is planar.**

