mastery.question_text:
This floor plan is drawn with no doorways crossing.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\coordinate (A) at (2.0,3.2);
\coordinate (B) at (3.8,2.2);
\coordinate (C) at (3.8,0.2);
\coordinate (D) at (2.0,-0.8);
\coordinate (E) at (0.2,0.2);
\coordinate (F) at (0.2,2.2);
\draw (A)--(B);
\draw (B)--(C);
\draw (C)--(D);
\draw (D)--(E);
\draw (E)--(F);
\draw (F)--(A);
\draw (B)--(F);
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (C) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (D) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (E) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (F) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above] at (A) {$A$};
\node[right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[below] at (D) {$D$};
\node[left] at (E) {$E$};
\node[left] at (F) {$F$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
How many faces does this drawing have? A new doorway is then built joining $C$ and $E$, drawn inside the plan without crossing any existing doorway. How many faces does the new drawing have, and why?

mastery.solution_text:
The doorway $BF$ cuts off the region $ABF$, leaving the region $BCDEF$ below it.
$2+1$
$=3$
The new doorway $CE$ joins two rooms that both border the region $BCDEF$, so drawing it inside splits that one region into $CDE$ and $BCEF$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\coordinate (A) at (2.0,3.2);
\coordinate (B) at (3.8,2.2);
\coordinate (C) at (3.8,0.2);
\coordinate (D) at (2.0,-0.8);
\coordinate (E) at (0.2,0.2);
\coordinate (F) at (0.2,2.2);
\draw (A)--(B);
\draw (B)--(C);
\draw (C)--(D);
\draw (D)--(E);
\draw (E)--(F);
\draw (F)--(A);
\draw (B)--(F);
\draw (C)--(E);
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (C) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (D) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (E) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (F) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above] at (A) {$A$};
\node[right] at (B) {$B$};
\node[right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[below] at (D) {$D$};
\node[left] at (E) {$E$};
\node[left] at (F) {$F$};
\node at (2.0,2.4) {$1$};
\node at (2.0,1.2) {$2$};
\node at (2.0,-0.1) {$3$};
\node at (4.6,3.0) {$4$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
An extra edge drawn inside one face always divides that face into two, so the count rises by exactly $1$.
**The original drawing has $3$ faces and the new drawing has $4$.**

mastery.question_text:
Sam is shown the crossing-free circuit below and makes two statements.
$1$. "The drawing has $4$ faces, because $4$ regions are fenced in by tracks."
$2$. "If I redraw this circuit so that two tracks cross, the circuit becomes non-planar."
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (2.0,0);
\coordinate (C) at (4.0,0);
\coordinate (D) at (4.0,2.2);
\coordinate (E) at (2.0,2.2);
\coordinate (F) at (0,2.2);
\draw (A)--(B);
\draw (B)--(C);
\draw (C)--(D);
\draw (D)--(E);
\draw (E)--(F);
\draw (F)--(A);
\draw (B)--(E);
\draw (A)--(E);
\draw (B)--(D);
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (C) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (D) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (E) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (F) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[below] at (B) {$B$};
\node[below right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[above right] at (D) {$D$};
\node[above] at (E) {$E$};
\node[above left] at (F) {$F$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Explain what is wrong with each statement.

mastery.solution_text:
Statement $1$: the four fenced-in regions are only part of the answer, because the unbounded region outside the whole drawing is a face as well.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.0]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (2.0,0);
\coordinate (C) at (4.0,0);
\coordinate (D) at (4.0,2.2);
\coordinate (E) at (2.0,2.2);
\coordinate (F) at (0,2.2);
\draw (A)--(B);
\draw (B)--(C);
\draw (C)--(D);
\draw (D)--(E);
\draw (E)--(F);
\draw (F)--(A);
\draw (B)--(E);
\draw (A)--(E);
\draw (B)--(D);
\fill (A) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (B) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (C) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (D) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (E) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (F) circle (2.5pt);
\node[below left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[below] at (B) {$B$};
\node[below right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[above right] at (D) {$D$};
\node[above] at (E) {$E$};
\node[above left] at (F) {$F$};
\node at (1.4,0.6) {$1$};
\node at (0.6,1.5) {$2$};
\node at (3.4,0.6) {$3$};
\node at (2.6,1.5) {$4$};
\node at (4.6,2.8) {$5$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$4+1$
$=5$
Statement $2$: planar describes the network, not one drawing of it. A network is planar as soon as **one** crossing-free drawing exists, and drawing it badly afterwards cannot take that away.
**The drawing has $5$ faces because the outer region counts, and drawing the circuit badly does not make a planar circuit non-planar.**

